          "Remember our crucial modeling trick from Day 41: if gateway node `hub` caps out at 50 Gbps CPU forwarding throughput despite two incoming fiber cables carrying 40 Gbps and 35 Gbps (totaling 75 Gbps), a plain edge graph ignores that choke point! Splitting vertex `hub` into `hub_in -> hub_out` with capacity 50 models hardware ceilings faithfully.",
          "During Dinic execution, BFS constructs a strict shortest-path DAG (`level[v] == level[u] + 1`) while DFS uses current-arc pointer `ptr[u]` pruning. Because `ptr[u]` permanently advances past dead-end outgoing edges inside each phase, blocking flow finishes without repeating dead walks.",
          "Immediately after the final BFS returns `False` because no augmenting path reaches sink `T`, the final `level[]` array bifurcates the entire graph cleanly: every vertex with `level[u] != -1` sits on the source side, and every unreachable vertex with `level[v] == -1` sits on the sink side.",
          "Every forward original edge crossing from reachable `u` to unreachable `v` belongs to the exact Minimum Cut, proving mathematically which link or router upgrade unlocks aggregate throughput! Comparing `max_gbps >= target_gbps` creates a clean SLA admission gate.",
