          "Suppose a crawler or database gateway needs to reject duplicate URLs or missing keys across 100 MILLION entries using only a tiny fraction of memory (`~1.2 bytes` per item instead of `64 bytes` per raw hash key!). A **Bloom Filter** trades a tunable false-positive probability for 50x memory compression — with ZERO false negatives ever!",
          "Under the hood, a Bloom Filter allocates one bit array of `m` bits (stored compactly inside one Python integer `bitset = 0` or `bytearray`) plus `k` independent hash functions mapping each key onto indices in `[0, m - 1]`. Inserting `key` sets all `k` bits ON (`bitset |= (1 << pos)`). Querying `key` checks whether ALL `k` bits equal `1`!",
          "Look at the ironclad asymmetry: if ANY of the `k` bits equals `0`, that key was **100% DEFINITELY NEVER INSERTED** (0% false negatives!). If all `k` bits equal `1`, that key was *probably* seen — though unrelated hashes could collide and flip those bits by chance (`false positive`).",
          "CRITICAL Python engineering trap: NEVER use built-in `hash(key)` inside persistent or reproducible Bloom Filters! Python randomizes `PYTHONHASHSEED` on every process restart for HashDoS defense, scrambling built-in `hash()` values completely across runs!",
          "Instead, compute ONE deterministic cryptographic `hashlib.sha256` digest per key, slice two 64-bit hashes `(h1, h2)`, and apply the classic **Kirsch-Mitzenmacher formula**: `pos_j = (h1 + j * h2) % m` for `j = 0 .. k - 1` — getting `k` fast reproducible hash indices from ONE SHA-256 call! Test it below."
