          "Graham Cormode and S. Muthukrishnan's **Count-Min Sketch (CMS)** solves frequency tracking cleanly in fixed `d x w` integer counters! Think of CMS as a `d`-row matrix of width `w` buckets (`e.g., 5 rows x 256 columns = 5 KB RAM total!`), where each row `r` runs its OWN independent deterministic hash `h_r(key) in [0, w - 1]`.",
          "Every `add(key, delta=1)` update increments ALL `d` counters across rows: `table[r][h_r(key)] += delta` (`O(d)` = constant time!). Look carefully at what happens upon querying `query(key)`: because every count `delta >= 0` is strictly NON-NEGATIVE, collisions from unrelated keys can ONLY INFLATE a cell (`table[r][h_r(key)] >= true_count`), NEVER subtract!",
          "Therefore, across all `d` independent hash rows, EVERY single bucket is an upper bound (`>= true_count`), so returning `min(table[r][h_r(key)] for r in range(d))` selects the LEAST-polluted bucket across all rows — guaranteeing ZERO undercounting while suppressing collision noise exponentially with rows `d`!",
