          "We covered uniform sampling (Algorithm R), membership (Bloom Filter), and frequency (Count-Min Sketch). What about the fourth streaming pillar: counting **UNIQUE** items (`COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)`) without storing every unseen ID in a giant hash `set`?",
          "Notice the brilliant probabilistic clue hidden in uniform random 32-bit hashes: roughly `1/2` start with `>= 0` leading zeros, `1/4` begin `01...`, `1/8` begin `001...`, and `1/2^k` begin with `k - 1` leading zeros! Therefore, spotting a hash starting with `9` leading zeros suggests around `2^10 = 1,024` distinct hashes passed through!",
          "Taking a single global maximum suffers high variance (one lucky `00000000...` blows up the estimate!). **HyperLogLog (Flajolet-Martin / Durand)** cuts variance cleanly by dividing elements using their leading `b` hash bits into `m = 2^b` independent buckets (`registers[bucket]`), tracking the longest zero run seen in each tiny 6-bit register, and combining buckets with the **Harmonic Mean** (so one lucky outlier cannot hijack all 64 buckets!).",
          "Think about that memory miracle: tracking `m = 64` small integer registers (`64 bytes` total RAM!) tracks thousands of distinct user IDs within a tiny percent margin!",
